#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d887d9f-b9c4-54b3-a53e-691989bda962" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Odd Functions Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "66135849-b0f8-53a9-aab8-4b6aebde3cd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3994" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6c7717b81ff0b7af12e3a247ecee69bb61bdd79fdea79ac5a7e769e4a3f1fd4.png)\n\nWhich of the above show the graph of an odd function?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs I, II, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n- On the other hand, graph III does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph $180^\\circ$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph III shows an odd function.\n\n![](images/9e/9eb4d72e1fbcc0fdefbca6301127be3bb98c6a7a8bc1b4bffb66144fe9c15010.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff4063-9e51-53fa-ab55-e68540e6f587" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Given the Table for an Even or Odd Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5b22df97-b731-5d77-a6ed-2a90efcd95a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3996" :question/problem "The table below defines the function $g(x)$. Given that $g(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b$.\n\n| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $20$ | $11$ | $-4$ | $a-3$ | $11$ | $b+5$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $g(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $g(-x)=g(x)$.\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\ng(-1) = g(1)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(-1) &= g(1) \\\\\n- 4 &= a - 3 \\\\\na &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\ng(-5) = g(5)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(-5) &= g(5) \\\\\n20 &= b + 5 \\\\\nb &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b = -1 + 15 = 14\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb0d0f84-a628-5605-a75f-f4f5459e047c" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even Functions Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "f8d678e6-c0db-534d-9ae2-0551ea8201bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4172" :question/problem "![](images/24/24c2b0f28f2ae9eae92527191f0e97fb87aa1eb74323cf2d4b7f2f5fc2ecdd71.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of an even function?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n- Graphs II, III, and IV are not symmetric about the $y$-axis. So, they are **not** even functions.\n- On the other hand, graph I is symmetric about the $y$-axis. That is to say, if we reflect graph I over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that graph I is an even function.\n\n![](images/b2/b2598584b088e924dc518aa39d4adb1966f5670c827389c90bbf089dc50ee5e8.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fa847d9-f0bf-57fb-92b2-27c8a7cdb3b1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even and Odd Functions Using the Algebraic Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "373bdf54-aaa4-5e94-82a7-95c0a396623a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4183" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x)= \\mid x \\mid -1$ even, odd, both, or neither?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x$, while\n- a function is odd if $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x$.\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= \\begin{vmatrix}(-x) & -1 \\\\ = & x\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nthe function is even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-f(x) &= - (\\begin{vmatrix}x & -1) \\\\ = - & x\\end{vmatrix}+ 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-x) \\neq -f(x)\n$$\n\nthe function is not odd.\n\nTherefore, the function is only even."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d887d9f-b9c4-54b3-a53e-691989bda962" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Odd Functions Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4f58ee5-0c36-56ee-9bd7-5331eac75033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080976} {:answer/id #uuid "c353ceba-981d-58bc-95fb-a85a4e6c5554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080977} {:answer/id #uuid "fff7af82-2425-574c-bda3-d8fd8a75f3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080978} {:answer/id #uuid "08cbbf4d-ea7a-56f2-87fa-ab9db45db4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080979} {:answer/id #uuid "40b0685d-6564-51cf-9721-c6c8cad9d40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080980}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3dcd9337-742e-5c39-9083-01813135fd97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080975}] :question/id #uuid "f0f88a98-a327-50e8-b4f1-ca167776d9e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-36558" :question/problem "![](images/92/92436599a8df12b4121e293adc861075d0bc8c7b31628922a7c2d8e2dad1cfef.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?"}]}
